    "q-269510": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269510",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x,\n$$\n\nwhere the dot denotes matrix multiplication. What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
